# The differences between the exported surface of every published module and the surface of its last
# tag, one line per object.
#
#   module ~ class ~ object ~ change ~ disposition ~ reason
#
# Read by .dev/validate/apidiff.sh. This is not a suppression list: every line is a claim the run has to
# satisfy. A difference with no line here fails the check, and a line whose difference no longer occurs
# fails it just as loudly — the change it files is gone, or a release moved the tag past it, so the line
# has to go. A line whose class or change text no longer matches what the run measures fails too: the row
# then describes a different fact than the one that occurs, and has to be rewritten. That is what keeps
# the file the measured list rather than a remembered one.
#
# The class and the change are apidiff's own words, measured, never asserted:
#
#   incompatible  a signature changed, a symbol or package was removed, a constant's value changed, a
#                 type lost comparability, an interface a third party may implement grew a method, or a
#                 named type changed identity (apidiff prints `changed from T to T` when the text is the
#                 same and the type behind it is not, such as a type that became an alias of a type in
#                 another package). Each of these is a decision a release has to make before its tag.
#
#   compatible    an addition. Allowed on a minor release, not on a patch one — which is what makes a row
#                 of this class on a frozen major a finding rather than routine.
#
# The disposition is the decision the row carries:
#
#   keep          ships on this major — backwards compatible, a security repair, or a defect repair whose
#                 remedy cannot be compatible. Has to say why.
#   cut           leaves this major before its next tag, and lives on the next major. Has to say why.
#   unclassified  measured, not yet decided. The reason may be empty, because "measured, undecided" is the
#                 whole claim, and a generic reason would be a reason nobody can review. The gate counts
#                 these rows: that number is the work left before a tag.
#
# The tag each module is compared with is deliberately not a field. The run reads it from git and prints
# it beside the module, so this file cannot carry a version that has gone stale. Every field is compared
# for exact equality, so nothing here is a pattern: a `*` in an object is a pointer receiver in apidiff's
# own spelling, never a wildcard. A `~` inside a field is refused, being the separator. Fields are
# trimmed, so the columns may be aligned.
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Seeded on 2026-09-04 from the branch fix/v3-stabilization-sweep at a8ae204: 312 rows on v3 against
# v3.13.0 (101 incompatible, 211 compatible), 90 rows on the v3 integrations against their last tags,
# and none on v1, v2 or their integrations, whose unreleased changes are patch-level by measurement.
# Every row landed unclassified except the two on the message bus transport's Close, adjudicated before
# this band existed. The rows are the work list of the step that cuts from v3 what is not backwards
# compatible before v3.14.0; a decided row replaces `unclassified` with `keep` or `cut` and a reason.
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

integrations/amqp/v3 ~ incompatible ~ (*Transport).Close ~ changed from func(github.com/precision-soft/melody/v3/runtime/contract.Runtime) error to func() error ~ keep ~ the amqp transport follows the messagebus contract's Close, which dropped its Runtime parameter so the container's ordered teardown can reach it; same adjudication as the framework row, shipping on the next amqp tag as a documented break
integrations/amqp/v3 ~ compatible ~ ServerSentEventBackplaneConfig.CallTimeout ~ added ~ keep ~ bounds each stretch of a publish attempt — the wait for its turn and the write, which the amqp client runs with its context discarded; a broker that stopped reading held the write, every later broadcast and the hub shutdown for good, and v3.14.0 is the last minor that ships features
integrations/amqp/v3 ~ compatible ~ (*ServerSentEventBackplane).CloseWithContext ~ added ~ keep ~ the optional context-taking close the container's teardown prefers, so the budget an operator declares reaches the stretches this component spends instead of stopping at a constant it invented; Close stays as the door for a caller with no budget, and v3.14.0 is the last minor that ships features
integrations/amqp/v3 ~ compatible ~ TransportConfig.PublishTimeout ~ added ~ keep ~ bounds the write of one send for the same reason the backplane field exists; the confirmation wait after the write is bounded by it too, and v3.14.0 is the last minor that ships features
integrations/amqp/v3 ~ compatible ~ (*Transport).CloseWithContext ~ added ~ keep ~ the optional context-taking close the container's teardown prefers, so the budget an operator declares reaches the stretches this component spends instead of stopping at a constant it invented; Close stays as the door for a caller with no budget, and v3.14.0 is the last minor that ships features
integrations/awss3/v3 ~ compatible ~ Config.String ~ added ~ keep ~ redacts AccessKey and SecretKey on every fmt verb — both are exported fields a plain %v prints in the clear; a defect repair mirroring the encrypt key provider, and v3.14.0 is the last minor that ships features
integrations/awss3/v3 ~ compatible ~ Config.Format ~ added ~ keep ~ answers the numeric verbs fmt never routes through Stringer with the same redacted String rendering, so no verb reflection-walks the secret fields
integrations/bunorm/migrate/v3 ~ compatible ~ SetDefaultRunnerOption ~ added ~ keep ~ the process-default door bun's fixed (ctx, db) migration signature needs to receive the command's writer and colour posture, installed only for the length of a run; a compatible addition, and v3.14.0 is the last minor that ships features
